        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{4x-8}+\\frac{1}{4-4x})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{4x-8}+\\frac{1}{4-4x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4x-8}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{4-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|4x-8|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|4-4x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|\\frac{4x-8}{4-4x}|+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K|\\frac{4x-8}{4-4x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm.",
